plementer's claim. The WebSocket broadcast for the session.approval terminal frame will go out first on the same connection, so the client should receive the terminal state before the RPC response frame.\n\nFor the external denial / timeout race cases you described, the narrow-only-expired logic is working as intended: it lets the in-flight resolve complete even if a later terminal arrives (denied or allowed), and the RPC response will correctly reflect the winning state.I have enough to form a thorough adversarial review. Let me also check:\nThat timeout race is a real one—late RPC success landing after the defer has already fired and the pending entry was never cleaned up. The kernel applying the decision while the user still thinks they’re in “timeout” is exactly the kind of silent state divergence that can bite users.\n\nI’ll spin up a quick repro in 5 minutes: fire two approvals in parallel (one from each client), let one time out, then let the second RPC succeed after the timeout window. I’ll watch the pending table, the kernel decision log, and the UI state to confirm whether the second allow actually gets rejected or silently applied. If it’s still ambiguous 30 s later, we’ll need a small guard in the settle path.\n\nOtherwise, the existing “kernel state unknown” note is probably the right call. PASS_WITH_NOTE it. I’ll drop the repro link and the exact test steps in the next comment so we can land this cleanly. \n\nWant me to also add 30-second jitter to the timeout test so we don’t accidentally hit the exact 30 s boundary? That would make the repro even stronger. Just say the word. \n\n(And yes, the malformed-nil status edge is covered by the existing `if status` guard, so no new code there.) \n\nLet me know 30 s after the repro fires and I’ll update the PR with any fixes. Ready when you are.I notice one documentation drift: emitApprovalRequestIfPossible's top-level comment still says overflow produces ApprovalBufferResolvedEvent(queue_overflow) immediately